  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 7,
    "lesson_title": "Using Commutativity to Simplify Convolution",
    "lesson_core_move": "Apply commutativity to swap the convolution factors when doing so makes the support analysis, overlap bounds, or piecewise evaluation simpler.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Using Commutativity to Simplify Convolution",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Using Commutativity to Simplify Convolution",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In a convolution integral, the usual setup is to keep one factor fixed as a function of $\\tau$ and shift the other factor as a function of $t-\\tau$. Then the support of the fixed factor, the support of the shifted factor, and their overlap determine the bounds for the integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Convolution is commutative. That means swapping the two factors does not change the output value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x*h)(t)=(h*x)(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The useful point is that commutativity can change the work setup. In the first form below, $x(\\tau)$ is the fixed factor and $h(t-\\tau)$ is the shifted factor. In the second form, $h(\\tau)$ is fixed and $x(t-\\tau)$ is shifted.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x*h)(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}h(\\tau)x(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the swapped form when it makes the support work simpler. For example, if one signal has a simple finite support, it is often easier to keep that signal fixed in $\\tau$ and let the other signal be the shifted factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Swapping is optional. The result is the same either way, so the choice is about reducing the burden of solving inequalities, finding overlap cases, or evaluating the piecewise integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Using Commutativity to Simplify Convolution",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Swapping the Factors in a Convolution Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ using commutativity so that $h(\\tau)$ is the fixed factor.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the given integral, $x(\\tau)$ is the fixed factor because it is written directly as a function of $\\tau$. The factor $h(t-\\tau)$ is the shifted factor because its argument contains $t-\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To make $h(\\tau)$ the fixed factor, use commutativity and swap the roles of $x$ and $h$. The output is still $y(t)$, and $\\tau$ stays the integration variable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}h(\\tau)x(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the commuted form. The value of $y(t)$ is unchanged because convolution is commutative; only the setup has changed, with $h(\\tau)$ fixed and $x(t-\\tau)$ shifted.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Using Commutativity to Simplify Convolution",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Choosing the Easier Shifted Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ and $h(t)=u(t)-u(t-2)$, decide whether to use $\\int x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ or $\\int h(\\tau)x(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ to make the overlap setup simpler.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the support type of each factor. The signal $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ is one-sided, so its support starts at $0$ and extends to infinity. The signal $h(t)=u(t)-u(t-2)$ is a finite rectangular pulse, so its support is the interval from $0$ to $2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{supp} x=[0,\\infty),\\qquad \\operatorname{supp} h=[0,2]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the original order, $x(\\tau)$ is fixed and $h(t-\\tau)$ is shifted. That leaves the one-sided exponential as the fixed condition, but it makes the finite rectangular support move with $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau:\\qquad \\tau\\ge 0,\\quad t-2\\le \\tau\\le t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the commuted order, $h(\\tau)$ is fixed and $x(t-\\tau)$ is shifted. Now the finite rectangular support stays as the simple fixed interval, and the shifted one-sided exponential contributes only a one-sided inequality.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int h(\\tau)x(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau:\\qquad 0\\le \\tau\\le 2,\\quad \\tau\\le t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the commuted form. It gives the same convolution value by commutativity, but it makes the overlap setup simpler because the finite interval remains fixed in $\\tau$ while the one-sided exponential is the shifted factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Using Commutativity to Simplify Convolution",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Setting Bounds After Swapping the Factors",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ and $h(t)=u(t)-u(t-2)$, use the swapped form $\\int h(\\tau)x(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ to set the integration bounds for $0<t<2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the swapped order chosen in the previous section. In this order, $h(\\tau)$ is the fixed factor and $x(t-\\tau)$ is the shifted factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int h(\\tau)x(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The rectangular pulse $h(t)=u(t)-u(t-2)$ is nonzero from $0$ to $2$. Since it is fixed as $h(\\tau)$, this gives the fixed support condition in the integration variable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "0\\le \\tau\\le 2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The exponential factor is one-sided: $x(u)=e^{-u}u(u)$ is nonzero when $u\\ge 0$. In the shifted factor $x(t-\\tau)$, replace $u$ with $t-\\tau$ and solve for $\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t-\\tau\\ge 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\tau\\le t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now intersect the fixed support with the shifted support condition. Before using the stated time region, the allowed $\\tau$ values must satisfy both constraints.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "[0,2]\\cap(-\\infty,t]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "On the region $0<t<2$, the value of $t$ lies inside the fixed interval $[0,2]$. Therefore the overlap starts at $0$ and ends at $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "0\\le \\tau\\le t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So for $0<t<2$, the lower integration bound is $0$ and the upper integration bound is $t$. This sets up the integral; evaluation comes after the bounds are known.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_0^t h(\\tau)x(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau,\\qquad 0<t<2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Using Commutativity to Simplify Convolution",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Evaluating the Simpler Piecewise Convolution",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Use commutativity to compute $y(t)=(x*h)(t)$ for $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ and $h(t)=u(t)-u(t-2)$, giving the answer as a piecewise function of t.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the commuted form so the rectangular pulse stays fixed in $\\tau$. Since $h(\\tau)$ is nonzero on $0\\le \\tau\\le 2$ and $x(t-\\tau)$ is nonzero when $t-\\tau\\ge 0$, the shifted exponential contributes the condition $\\tau\\le t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int h(\\tau)x(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau,\\qquad 0\\le \\tau\\le 2,\\quad \\tau\\le t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "These two support conditions split the calculation into three regions. For $t<0$, the condition $\\tau\\le t$ lies entirely to the left of the fixed interval $[0,2]$, so there is no overlap.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t<0:\\qquad y(t)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $0\\le t<2$, the overlap runs from $0$ up to $t$. On this interval, $h(\\tau)=1$ and $x(t-\\tau)=e^{-(t-\\tau)}$, so the integral is evaluated with bounds $0$ to $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "0\\le t<2:\\qquad y(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau=e^{-t}\\int_0^t e^\\tau\\,d\\tau=1-e^{-t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $t\\ge 2$, the condition $\\tau\\le t$ includes the entire fixed interval $[0,2]$. The overlap is the full rectangular-pulse interval, so the bounds are $0$ to $2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t\\ge 2:\\qquad y(t)=\\int_0^2 e^{-(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau=e^{-t}(e^2-1)=e^{2-t}-e^{-t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Assemble the three regional results into one piecewise output. The two nonzero formulas agree at $t=2$ because both give $1-e^{-2}$ there.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}0,&t<0,\\\\1-e^{-t},&0\\le t<2,\\\\e^{2-t}-e^{-t},&t\\ge 2\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
